* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* CREATE.ACCOUNT USER  <name> {SSH | API | BOTH | NONE} {SH-ON} {OS-ON} {VOC-ON} {BP-ON} {DICT-ON} {SHARED-READ-OFF} {SHARED-WRITE-ON} {NET-ON} {FILES-ON} {NO.QUERY}
* CREATE.ACCOUNT GROUP <name> {NO.QUERY}
* CREATE.ACCOUNT OTHER <name> <pathname> {NO.QUERY}
*
* There are no tiers: every account gets the one VOC layer (NEWVOC).  What an account may DO beyond
*   its own SD work is four per-account gates, each OFF unless SDSYS turns it on (owner, 13 Oct 2026:
*   Core ships with high security and the administrator loosens it by choice):
*     SSH, API    the two remote doors; SSH, API or BOTH names the ones wanted (NONE is the default)
*     SH-ON       the SH verb and "!"
*     OS-ON       OS.EXECUTE, and OPENSEQ / OPENPATH on a path outside the account's own directory
*     VOC-ON, BP-ON, DICT-ON   (17 Oct 26, S.64) saving changes to a VOC, to a program file (and compiling and
*                 cataloguing), to a dictionary; each its own switch, loaded at the person's sign-on
*     SHARED-READ-OFF, SHARED-WRITE-ON   (18 Oct 26, S.66) a new user account can open and read the files of the shared account by
*                 name unless SHARED-READ-OFF is typed, and cannot write them unless SHARED-WRITE-ON is typed.  The only two shared
*                 words here: the other two (SHARED-READ-ON, SHARED-WRITE-OFF) restate the default and are not accepted.  A GROUP or OTHER
*                 account takes them too, written only when asked.
*     NET-ON      (17 Oct 26, S.65) opening a file on another SD server (SDNet); account-scoped like SD.ACCOUNTS
*     FILES-ON    (17 Oct 26, S.65) a files-only login: the person joins the Linux group sdfiles, which lets an SDNet client open and work with
*                 files and do nothing else; sdapi, when also given, still gives the whole API.  A user account only (10087, 10105)
*     FILES-WRITE-ON (18 Oct 26, S.65) with FILES-ON: the person also joins sdfileswrite, so that files-only login may CHANGE files on this
*                 server (write, delete, lock, clear); without it it can read.  Off unless typed.
*     FILES-ADMIN-ON (21 Oct 26, S.73 item 3) with FILES-ON and FILES-WRITE-ON: the person also joins sdfilesadmin, so that files-only login may
*                 ADMINISTER files on this server (configure, index, create, delete, the file's counter).  Off unless typed.
*     AI-ON       (23 Oct 26, S.69 step 2) the person joins the Linux group sdaiusers: the right to use the AI provider's API through the helper sd-ai (one provider and
*                 model for every account, chosen by SDSYS with set.ai).  Stands alone; off unless typed; a user account only (10087, 10081).
*   SH-ON and OS-ON are two independent switches, so "SH-ON OS-ON" gives both and neither cancels the
*   other.  A GROUP or OTHER account has no Linux user of its own, so it is refused all four (10087,
*   10105).  MODIFY.ACCOUNT changes any of them later, either way.  ATTACH is install-only and says only
*   that the Linux user already exists: the installing user's own account starts off like any other.
*
* 09 Sep 26 dm - THIS BLOCK WAS STALE AND IT COST SOMETHING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS
*   CORRECTED RATHER THAN JUST EXTENDED.  It read
*   "CREATE.ACCOUNT account.name path.name [NO.QUERY]", the pre-0.9.0 form:
*   USER/GROUP/OTHER has been required since rev 0.9.0 and only OTHER takes a
*   pathname.  A session writing message 10033 copied this line, shipped
*   "CREATE.ACCOUNT name path ADMINISTRATOR" as advice to an administrator, and
*   the owner caught it - the command would have failed if typed.  ***THE
*   SYNTAX THE VERB PRINTS AT :246 IS THE TRUTH; THIS BLOCK IS COMMENTARY AND
*   HAD DRIFTED.***  The port's copy of this same block is stale in the same
*   way - do not take it from there either.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $create.account
$catalog $CREATEA

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h
$include createacc.h

* 01 Oct 26 dm - S.50: THIS VERB NOW ONLY READS THE SENTENCE.  Everything from the
*   register check on is !create_account, so RESTORE.ACCOUNT can make a missing
*   account without building a sentence; the settings it is given are exactly
*   what the words below set (createacc.h).  The comments that explained each
*   step moved with the code.

   prompt ''

* rev 0.9.0 create-account requires admin priv, are we admin?
   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

   parser = "!PARSER"
   settings = ''

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword) ;* Verb create-account
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.type, keyword)
   acc.type = upcase(acc.type)

* 11 Sep 26 dm - THE ONE-SHOT MARKER THAT MAKES ATTACH INSTALL-ONLY (PORT_ADOPTION
*   15).  installsdcore.sh writes @sdsys/$attach.<name> immediately before running
*   the verb.  It is named per account as soon as the name is parsed, so one
*   marker authorises one account; nothing writes the bare name.  ***IT IS NOT
*   SHIPPED AND MUST NEVER BE.***  more.args accepts ATTACH only while the marker
*   exists and the session is -internal; !create_account checks both again and
*   consumes the marker.
   attach.marker = @sdsys:@ds:'$attach'
   begin case
   case acc.type = 'USER'
*    CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <user acct name> {SSH | API | BOTH | NONE} {SH-ON} {OS-ON} {NO.QUERY}
     call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
     if token.type = PARSER$END then stop
     attach.marker = @sdsys:@ds:'$attach.':downcase(acc.name)
     gosub more.args

   case acc.type = 'GROUP'
*    CREATE.ACCOUNT GROUP <grp acct name> {NO.QUERY}
     call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
     if token.type = PARSER$END then stop
     gosub more.args

   case acc.type = 'OTHER'
*  CREATE.ACCOUNT OTHER <acc name> <path> {NO.QUERY}
     call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
     if token.type = PARSER$END then stop
     call @parser(parser$get.token, token.type, pathname, keyword)
     if token.type = PARSER$END then stop
     settings<CA$PATH> = trim(pathname)
     gosub more.args

   case 1
    crt 'Command Syntax: create.account user <user name> {ssh | api | both | none} {sh-on} {os-on} {voc-on} {bp-on} {dict-on} {shared-read-off} {shared-write-on} {net-on} {files-on} {files-write-on} {files-admin-on} {ai-on} {no.query}'
    crt '                create.account group <group name> {no.query}'
    crt '                create.account other <account name> <pathname> {no.query}'
    crt '  A new user account starts with no ssh, no API, no shell (sh), no os.execute, and no right to change'
    crt '  the VOC (voc-on), a program file (bp-on) or a dictionary (dict-on), no files on other SD servers (net-on),'
    crt '  and no files-only login (files-on; it cannot change files here without files-write-on); it can open and read the files of the shared account by name unless'
    crt '  shared-read-off is typed, and cannot write them unless shared-write-on is typed;'
    crt '  the words above give them, and modify.account changes any of them afterwards, either way.'
    @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
    stop
   end case

   call !create_account(acc.type, acc.name, settings, err)
   if err # '' then
      stop sysmsg(err<1,1>, err<1,2>, err<1,3>, err<1,4>)
   end

* 06 Oct 26 dm - PAL-15, parity audit J-3: an account created is on the audit
*   trail, with the same string on both ports.  Only after !create_account
*   reported no error.
* 07 Oct 26 dm - PAL-24, the owner's lower-case rule: the command words are lower case,
*   so create.account and type=user (acc.type is USER or GROUP inside create_account).
* 19 Oct 26 dm - THE GATES GRANTED AT CREATION ARE ON THE AUDIT LINE (owner, 19 Oct 2026: "create-account should record what gates were granted";
*   SDNet audit K8f).  modify.account audits each word it applies, but create.account user NAME files-on files-write-on made the same grants and left
*   one line that named none of them.  gates= lists, in lower case and in the order of the usage line, every word of the sentence that grants or
*   withdraws an access (the settings !create_account was given), and says none when there is none.  The sd-elevate group changes behind ssh, api and
*   the files words are still only in its own log.  The words up to type= are unchanged (PAL-15: the same string on both ports), so the new tail is
*   the Linux template and Windows follows.
   gates = ''
   if settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'ssh'
   if settings<CA$ROUTE.API> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'api'
   if settings<CA$OS.SH> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'sh-on'
   if settings<CA$OS.EXECUTE> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'os-on'
   if settings<CA$VOC.EDIT> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'voc-on'
   if settings<CA$BP.EDIT> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'bp-on'
   if settings<CA$DICT.EDIT> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'dict-on'
   if settings<CA$NET> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'net-on'
   if settings<CA$FILES> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'files-on'
   if settings<CA$FILESWRITE> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'files-write-on'
   if settings<CA$FILESADMIN> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'files-admin-on'
   if settings<CA$AI> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'ai-on'
   if settings<CA$SHARED.READ.OFF> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'shared-read-off'
   if settings<CA$SHARED.WRITE> = 1 then gates<-1> = 'shared-write-on'
   if gates = '' then gates = 'none' else gates = convert(@fm, ',', gates)
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'create.account account=' : acc.name : ' type=' : downcase(acc.type) : ' gates=' : gates)

   return

* ======================================================================
   * Check for further command arguments
more.args:
   loop
      call @parser(parser$get.token, token.type, token, keyword)
   until token.type = PARSER$END
      begin case
         case keyword = KW$NO.QUERY
            settings<CA$NO.QUERY> = 1

* 20 Sep 26 dm - S.29: SSH, API, BOTH and NONE name the state wanted, as
*   MODIFY.ACCOUNT's do.  Matched on token text, not through the KW$ table.  Any
*   of them on a GROUP account is refused by !create_account (10087).
         case upcase(token) = 'SSH'
            settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> = 1 ; settings<CA$ROUTE.API> = 0
         case upcase(token) = 'API'
            settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> = 0 ; settings<CA$ROUTE.API> = 1
         case upcase(token) = 'BOTH'
            settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> = 1 ; settings<CA$ROUTE.API> = 1
         case upcase(token) = 'NONE'
            settings<CA$ROUTE.SSH> = 0 ; settings<CA$ROUTE.API> = 0

* 13 Oct 26 dm - S.63: SH-ON and OS-ON, the Windows port's words (its CREATEA, 27 Aug 2026).  Two
*   independent switches, so "SH-ON OS-ON" is both and neither cancels.  Off unless typed.  Matched on the
*   token text: the parser reads a word with a dash in it as one token.  A GROUP or OTHER account given
*   either is refused by !create_account (10105).
         case upcase(token) = 'SH-ON'
            settings<CA$OS.SH> = 1
         case upcase(token) = 'OS-ON'
            settings<CA$OS.EXECUTE> = 1

* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.64: VOC-ON, BP-ON and DICT-ON, the edit gates.  Independent switches like the two above; off unless typed.
         case upcase(token) = 'VOC-ON'
            settings<CA$VOC.EDIT> = 1
         case upcase(token) = 'BP-ON'
            settings<CA$BP.EDIT> = 1
         case upcase(token) = 'DICT-ON'
            settings<CA$DICT.EDIT> = 1

* 18 Oct 26 dm - S.66: SHARED-READ-OFF and SHARED-WRITE-ON, the shared-account gates.  Only the two words that differ from the delivered
*   default (read ON, write OFF); MODIFY.ACCOUNT has all four.  Matched on token text.
         case upcase(token) = 'SHARED-READ-OFF'
            settings<CA$SHARED.READ.OFF> = 1
         case upcase(token) = 'SHARED-WRITE-ON'
            settings<CA$SHARED.WRITE> = 1

* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.65 slice 1: NET-ON, the gate for opening a file on another SD server (SDNet), account-scoped; off unless typed.  The
*   gate takes effect for the remote-file slices that follow and does nothing yet.
         case upcase(token) = 'NET-ON'
            settings<CA$NET> = 1

* 17 Oct 26 dm - S.65 slice 2c: FILES-ON, the files-only login (membership of the Linux group sdfiles; SDNet's route), off unless typed.  A
*   GROUP or OTHER account given it is refused by !create_account.
         case upcase(token) = 'FILES-ON'
            settings<CA$FILES> = 1

* 18 Oct 26 dm - S.65 write group: FILES-WRITE-ON, the person also joins sdfileswrite, so a files-only login may change files on this server.  Off
*   unless typed.  !create_account refuses it without FILES-ON (11523), and for a GROUP or OTHER account as it refuses FILES-ON.
         case upcase(token) = 'FILES-WRITE-ON'
            settings<CA$FILESWRITE> = 1

* 21 Oct 26 dm - S.73 item 3: FILES-ADMIN-ON, the person also joins sdfilesadmin, so a files-only login may administer files on this server.  Off unless typed.
*   !create_account refuses it without FILES-ON and FILES-WRITE-ON (11526), and for a GROUP or OTHER account as it refuses FILES-ON.
         case upcase(token) = 'FILES-ADMIN-ON'
            settings<CA$FILESADMIN> = 1

* 23 Oct 26 dm - S.69 step 2: AI-ON, the person joins sdaiusers, the right to use the AI provider's API.  Off unless typed.  !create_account refuses it for a GROUP or OTHER
*   account as it refuses FILES-ON, and looks for the group before anything is made.
         case upcase(token) = 'AI-ON'
            settings<CA$AI> = 1

* 11 Sep 26 dm - ATTACH (renamed from ADOPT 19 Sep).  ***THE TESTS ARE IN THE
*   CASE CONDITION, AND THAT IS THE POINT***: with either gate shut the word
*   falls through to "Unexpected token (ATTACH)", the answer an invented word
*   gets, so a refusal never confirms the keyword exists.
         case upcase(token) = 'ATTACH' and kernel(K$INTERNAL, -1) and ospath(attach.marker, OS$EXISTS)
            settings<CA$ATTACH> = 1

         case 1
            stop sysmsg(2018, token) ;* Unexpected token (xx)
      end case
   repeat
return

end

* END-CODE
